## DO NOT

- ❌ Modify `fix_parser_v2.go` logic without confirmation.
- ❌ Use `time.Parse()` for FIX timestamps (causes timezone bugs).
- ❌ Create 1 issue per message (violates aggregation).
- ❌ Modify `db_manager.go` schema without migration scripts.
- ❌ Default security booleans to `false`.
- ❌ **USE `appConfig.LogReceiver` in `api_service.go`** - Use `appConfig.ReliableReceiver` instead (see Rule #6).
- ❌ **Re-create existing functionality** - Always check MCP servers and existing codebase before building new tools.
- ❌ **Create new test programs** - Use existing test infrastructure and MCP tools instead of writing new scanning/analysis scripts.
- ❌ **Ignore available MCP servers** - All 7 installed MCP servers must be utilized when applicable:
  - memory-bank (project context)
  - ast-grep (code structure)
  - redis (data operations)
  - kafka-schema-reg-mcp (Schema Registry)
  - sequential-thinking (complex reasoning)
  - filesystem (bulk file ops)
  - docker (container commands)
- ❌ **Use terminal docker exec** - Always use `mcp_docker_run_command` instead of `docker exec` terminal commands.
- ❌ **Create extra documentation files** - Do NOT create `.md` files to explain things. Explain directly in console/response. Only create documentation files when explicitly requested by the user.
- ❌ **Delete compiled artifacts** - NEVER delete `.exe`, `.dll`, `.so`, or other build outputs after compiling. The user needs these files for deployment. Only clean up build artifacts if explicitly requested.
- ❌ **Create extra code/files** - Do NOT create migration SQL files, update documentation, or add extra features unless explicitly requested. Focus ONLY on what the user asks for.
